WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.594
In this lesson you'll learn how chat,

2
00:00:03.594 --> 00:00:07.530
Cowork, desktop apps and terminal agents

3
00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.736
differ in what they can reach,

4
00:00:09.736 --> 00:00:12.139
which computer the work runs on,

5
00:00:12.139 --> 00:00:15.262
and where your files and chats end up.

6
00:00:15.262 --> 00:00:18.525
The same model can sit behind every surface,

7
00:00:18.525 --> 00:00:20.928
so pick the surface by the output

8
00:00:20.928 --> 00:00:22.786
and what it must reach,

9
00:00:22.786 --> 00:00:25.015
and before anything private goes in,

10
00:00:25.015 --> 00:00:27.128
check which machine runs the work

11
00:00:27.128 --> 00:00:29.961
and where your files and chats go.

12
00:00:29.961 --> 00:00:31.749
Since the sixth of October,

13
00:00:31.749 --> 00:00:35.034
Pro and Max users who kept Cowork local

14
00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:37.287
find new tasks run in the cloud,

15
00:00:37.287 --> 00:00:40.015
and, says Anthropic's help center,

16
00:00:40.015 --> 00:00:41.443
you can't switch back.

17
00:00:41.443 --> 00:00:42.511
Same app.

18
00:00:42.511 --> 00:00:45.043
A different computer doing the work.

19
00:00:45.043 --> 00:00:47.702
Here's where this lesson sits on the map.

20
00:00:47.702 --> 00:00:49.362
We're in First Build,

21
00:00:49.362 --> 00:00:52.381
the district about How do I build my first thing,

22
00:00:52.381 --> 00:00:54.830
on the shelf called Your workbench.

23
00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.107
That's where everything

24
00:00:56.107 --> 00:00:59.614
in this lesson gets stored.

25
00:00:59.614 --> 00:01:02.308
Picture one worker: the model.

26
00:01:02.308 --> 00:01:04.862
It always thinks in the vendor's building.

27
00:01:04.862 --> 00:01:07.938
And your computer is a house of rooms.

28
00:01:07.938 --> 00:01:09.993
The worker can reach any room;

29
00:01:09.993 --> 00:01:14.301
what changes is which shelves it can touch.

30
00:01:14.301 --> 00:01:16.379
In Anthropic's desktop app,

31
00:01:16.379 --> 00:01:18.097
Chat is the front counter:

32
00:01:18.097 --> 00:01:21.174
conversation, with no access to your files.

33
00:01:21.174 --> 00:01:22.753
Cowork is the back office:

34
00:01:22.753 --> 00:01:24.959
you hand over a job and walk away.

35
00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:26.375
Code is your own workshop,

36
00:01:26.375 --> 00:01:29.417
working directly on your local files.

37
00:01:29.417 --> 00:01:32.203
Before agents, there was only the counter;

38
00:01:32.203 --> 00:01:35.826
the only privacy question was your chat log.

39
00:01:35.826 --> 00:01:38.113
So pick the room by what you want back,

40
00:01:38.113 --> 00:01:39.657
not by the logo.

41
00:01:39.657 --> 00:01:42.072
OpenAI draws the same lines:

42
00:01:42.072 --> 00:01:44.847
Chat while the answer is still taking shape,

43
00:01:44.847 --> 00:01:48.852
Work for documents, Codex for working software.

44
00:01:48.852 --> 00:01:51.256
Here the picture first breaks:

45
00:01:51.256 --> 00:01:53.961
it isn't always the same worker.

46
00:01:53.961 --> 00:01:56.596
In September, OpenAI shipped new models

47
00:01:56.596 --> 00:01:59.325
to Work and Codex, but not Chat.

48
00:01:59.325 --> 00:02:02.634
What always differs is the reach.

49
00:02:02.634 --> 00:02:06.001
Ethan Mollick adds the stakes: when they're low,

50
00:02:06.001 --> 00:02:08.474
the options are all at least fine;

51
00:02:08.474 --> 00:02:11.899
when they're high, use the most advanced model.

52
00:02:11.899 --> 00:02:13.989
Even the bill can differ:

53
00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:16.624
your plan's allowance, or,

54
00:02:16.624 --> 00:02:21.999
once you approve a saved API key,

55
00:02:21.999 --> 00:02:24.530
billing per token instead.

56
00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.921
That's the lesson on your meter.

57
00:02:27.921 --> 00:02:29.721
On the sixteenth of September,

58
00:02:29.721 --> 00:02:31.265
Anthropic announced Cowork

59
00:02:31.265 --> 00:02:34.144
and chat are merging into one Claude,

60
00:02:34.144 --> 00:02:37.163
rolling out on Pro and Max first.

61
00:02:37.163 --> 00:02:39.995
You type into one box; Claude works out

62
00:02:39.995 --> 00:02:42.677
whether you need a quick answer or a task.

63
00:02:42.677 --> 00:02:43.710
By default,

64
00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.578
merged Claude asks before taking an action.

65
00:02:46.578 --> 00:02:49.132
Your app may show three tabs or one box;

66
00:02:49.132 --> 00:02:52.012
the rooms behind it still differ.

67
00:02:52.012 --> 00:02:54.891
The back office moved across town.

68
00:02:54.891 --> 00:02:57.945
Anthropic's help center says new Cowork tasks

69
00:02:57.945 --> 00:03:01.846
on Pro and Max run in the cloud: an isolated,

70
00:03:01.846 --> 00:03:05.259
temporary environment on Anthropic's servers.

71
00:03:05.259 --> 00:03:06.420
For local work,

72
00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.764
Anthropic points you to Claude Code.

73
00:03:09.764 --> 00:03:12.504
Running locally isn't staying local, though:

74
00:03:12.504 --> 00:03:16.358
what it reads still goes to Anthropic's servers.

75
00:03:16.358 --> 00:03:19.876
Team and Enterprise keep local Cowork,

76
00:03:19.876 --> 00:03:21.873
with cloud in beta.

77
00:03:21.873 --> 00:03:24.473
A cloud task reaches your house only

78
00:03:24.473 --> 00:03:27.213
through a hatch: the folders you connected,

79
00:03:27.213 --> 00:03:29.047
while the app is open.

80
00:03:29.047 --> 00:03:31.822
ChatGPT's Work splits the same way:

81
00:03:31.822 --> 00:03:34.191
local work can use your files;

82
00:03:34.191 --> 00:03:36.222
cloud work can't.

83
00:03:36.222 --> 00:03:38.486
In May, Anthropic called Cowork

84
00:03:38.486 --> 00:03:40.820
a virtual machine on your computer;

85
00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.699
for Pro and Max, that's stale.

86
00:03:43.699 --> 00:03:45.313
Second break in the picture:

87
00:03:45.313 --> 00:03:47.822
rooms move without you.

88
00:03:47.822 --> 00:03:49.610
Claude Code, your workshop,

89
00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.199
runs one loop through many doors:

90
00:03:52.199 --> 00:03:54.393
the terminal, the desktop app,

91
00:03:54.393 --> 00:03:57.749
your code editor, the browser, even Slack.

92
00:03:57.749 --> 00:03:59.374
And the bench moves.

93
00:03:59.374 --> 00:04:02.508
Local: your machine, full access.

94
00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:04.911
Cloud: virtual machines

95
00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:07.872
Anthropic or your organization runs.

96
00:04:07.872 --> 00:04:09.939
Remote Control: your machine,

97
00:04:09.939 --> 00:04:11.599
steered from a browser.

98
00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:16.243
The desktop app offers Local, Cloud, SSH or WSL.

99
00:04:16.243 --> 00:04:19.784
WSL is a Linux guest room inside Windows;

100
00:04:19.784 --> 00:04:22.071
SSH, a line to another machine,

101
00:04:22.071 --> 00:04:24.985
where the app installs Claude Code itself.

102
00:04:24.985 --> 00:04:27.632
Ethan Mollick adds a second axis:

103
00:04:27.632 --> 00:04:30.686
Codex and Claude Code expose the work itself;

104
00:04:30.686 --> 00:04:34.577
Cowork and Work hand back a finished result.

105
00:04:34.577 --> 00:04:37.375
The terminal is the workshop's speaking tube:

106
00:04:37.375 --> 00:04:38.826
just the window.

107
00:04:38.826 --> 00:04:42.031
The shell is the program at the other end,

108
00:04:42.031 --> 00:04:43.865
reading each line.

109
00:04:43.865 --> 00:04:47.534
Anthropic's terminal guide says Windows has two,

110
00:04:47.534 --> 00:04:49.798
PowerShell and CMD.

111
00:04:49.798 --> 00:04:52.654
PowerShell's prompt starts with PS;

112
00:04:52.654 --> 00:04:54.337
CMD's doesn't.

113
00:04:54.337 --> 00:04:56.183
A Mac uses zsh.

114
00:04:56.183 --> 00:04:58.459
One real line: echo first,

115
00:04:58.459 --> 00:05:00.990
double ampersand, echo second.

116
00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.023
CMD prints both.

117
00:05:03.474 --> 00:05:06.330
Windows PowerShell says the token double

118
00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:09.257
ampersand is not a valid statement separator.

119
00:05:09.257 --> 00:05:12.171
Same window, different shell, different meaning.

120
00:05:12.171 --> 00:05:15.191
Whatever goes down the tube runs

121
00:05:15.654 --> 00:05:18.080
as you, with your permissions.

122
00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.066
What to check first is the lesson

123
00:05:20.066 --> 00:05:21.934
on letting it loose.

124
00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:25.568
You'll see: claude is not recognized.

125
00:05:25.568 --> 00:05:27.298
That usually means the install

126
00:05:27.298 --> 00:05:29.956
folder isn't on your PATH yet.

127
00:05:29.956 --> 00:05:33.730
Recognise it; once Claude Code runs,

128
00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:37.677
/doctor checks and fixes its own setup.

129
00:05:37.677 --> 00:05:41.253
On Windows, the agent's shell may be Git Bash

130
00:05:41.253 --> 00:05:43.970
while yours is PowerShell, so a command

131
00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:46.953
copied from its trail can fail in yours.

132
00:05:46.953 --> 00:05:48.811
You may never open one:

133
00:05:48.811 --> 00:05:52.399
the desktop app runs commands without it.

134
00:05:52.399 --> 00:05:57.182
To go deep, MIT's Missing Semester still opens

135
00:05:57.182 --> 00:05:59.586
with two days of the shell.

136
00:05:59.586 --> 00:06:01.896
Sometimes the work gets a computer

137
00:06:01.896 --> 00:06:03.582
inside your computer.

138
00:06:04.044 --> 00:06:08.642
MIT's Missing Semester names two kinds.

139
00:06:08.642 --> 00:06:11.985
A virtual machine abstracts the entire computer,

140
00:06:11.985 --> 00:06:14.238
with its own operating system:

141
00:06:14.238 --> 00:06:17.222
a detached house on its own foundations.

142
00:06:17.222 --> 00:06:20.600
A container packages an app with its files

143
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.899
but shares the host's kernel:

144
00:06:22.899 --> 00:06:25.047
a flat in a shared building.

145
00:06:25.047 --> 00:06:27.740
Docker, the best-known container tool,

146
00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.704
was on Aaron's gap list.

147
00:06:30.109 --> 00:06:34.288
MIT notes Docker's isolation is weaker

148
00:06:34.288 --> 00:06:36.122
than a virtual machine's.

149
00:06:36.122 --> 00:06:39.071
Still, its exercises say an agent

150
00:06:39.071 --> 00:06:41.196
that skips approvals

151
00:06:41.196 --> 00:06:45.851
isn't secure run directly, but may be acceptable

152
00:06:45.851 --> 00:06:48.789
inside a virtual machine or container.

153
00:06:48.789 --> 00:06:53.028
That box is the lesson on sandboxes.

154
00:06:53.028 --> 00:06:56.151
A cloud session is the vendor's spare workshop:

155
00:06:56.151 --> 00:07:00.226
an isolated virtual machine Anthropic manages,

156
00:07:00.226 --> 00:07:03.454
still working after you close your laptop.

157
00:07:03.454 --> 00:07:07.680
claude --cloud clones your branch from GitHub,

158
00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.559
not the files on your bench, so push first.

159
00:07:10.559 --> 00:07:12.068
On Anthropic's hosting,

160
00:07:12.068 --> 00:07:14.948
your GitHub credentials never enter it.

161
00:07:14.948 --> 00:07:19.115
With no GitHub remote, or no Claude GitHub App,

162
00:07:19.115 --> 00:07:22.506
your project is bundled and uploaded instead.

163
00:07:22.506 --> 00:07:24.781
Untracked files stay home;

164
00:07:24.781 --> 00:07:27.765
committed ones go up, keys included.

165
00:07:27.765 --> 00:07:31.016
On a Mac, Linux or WSL,

166
00:07:31.016 --> 00:07:33.988
uncommitted edits to files named like keys,

167
00:07:33.988 --> 00:07:36.832
such as .env, stay too.

168
00:07:36.832 --> 00:07:39.328
On native Windows, uncommitted changes

169
00:07:39.328 --> 00:07:42.208
to tracked files upload whatever their name.

170
00:07:42.208 --> 00:07:44.565
Know what crosses.

171
00:07:44.565 --> 00:07:46.446
First, on Pro and Max,

172
00:07:46.446 --> 00:07:50.057
desktop no longer means on your computer:

173
00:07:50.057 --> 00:07:53.540
Cowork's work, including local files it opens,

174
00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:56.500
is processed on Anthropic's servers.

175
00:07:56.500 --> 00:08:00.506
A task fetches a copy of just the file it needs;

176
00:08:00.506 --> 00:08:03.838
delete the session and those copies go too,

177
00:08:03.838 --> 00:08:07.065
under Anthropic's retention practices.

178
00:08:07.065 --> 00:08:10.421
Close your laptop and Claude keeps going;

179
00:08:10.421 --> 00:08:13.962
scheduled tasks run with your computer off.

180
00:08:13.962 --> 00:08:15.831
Second, the hatch:

181
00:08:15.831 --> 00:08:18.454
any connected folder is reachable,

182
00:08:18.454 --> 00:08:22.042
and on a computer your organization manages,

183
00:08:22.042 --> 00:08:24.573
those are your employer's files.

184
00:08:24.573 --> 00:08:29.321
So Anthropic advises one dedicated working folder;

185
00:08:29.321 --> 00:08:30.982
Ethan Mollick connects only

186
00:08:30.982 --> 00:08:34.012
a non-private part of his Google Drive.

187
00:08:34.012 --> 00:08:38.518
The folder lesson's habit, carried across town.

188
00:08:38.518 --> 00:08:41.235
And every landlord keeps a logbook.

189
00:08:41.235 --> 00:08:42.698
First, training.

190
00:08:42.698 --> 00:08:44.892
Claude's Free, Pro and Max plans,

191
00:08:44.892 --> 00:08:47.725
Claude Code included, have a setting:

192
00:08:47.725 --> 00:08:50.279
Help improve our AI models.

193
00:08:50.279 --> 00:08:53.994
On, data can be kept up to five years; off,

194
00:08:53.994 --> 00:08:57.175
deleted chats are purged within thirty days.

195
00:08:57.175 --> 00:08:59.219
Pro is a consumer plan;

196
00:08:59.219 --> 00:09:03.212
Team, Enterprise and the API are commercial.

197
00:09:03.212 --> 00:09:04.884
Switching it off doesn't reach

198
00:09:04.884 --> 00:09:06.788
training already started.

199
00:09:06.788 --> 00:09:09.389
Second, memory is a separate store.

200
00:09:09.389 --> 00:09:10.689
Delete a chat;

201
00:09:10.689 --> 00:09:14.207
its memories stay until you delete them too.

202
00:09:14.207 --> 00:09:17.411
Third, keep work and home in separate accounts;

203
00:09:17.411 --> 00:09:19.907
shared logins aren't supported.

204
00:09:19.907 --> 00:09:21.324
Others differ:

205
00:09:21.324 --> 00:09:24.435
Google has humans review some Gemini chats,

206
00:09:24.435 --> 00:09:27.466
and keeps reviewed ones up to three years,

207
00:09:27.466 --> 00:09:30.287
even after you delete your activity.

208
00:09:30.287 --> 00:09:32.632
Ask any tool four questions.

209
00:09:32.632 --> 00:09:34.037
Is there a training setting?

210
00:09:34.037 --> 00:09:35.790
Do people review chats?

211
00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.472
How long is data kept with it off?

212
00:09:38.472 --> 00:09:40.376
What does deleting reach?

213
00:09:40.376 --> 00:09:42.117
The picture's last break:

214
00:09:42.117 --> 00:09:45.334
leaving a room doesn't take every copy with you.

215
00:09:45.334 --> 00:09:48.782
The agent decides whether a request needs a task,

216
00:09:48.782 --> 00:09:51.058
writes commands for its own shell,

217
00:09:51.058 --> 00:09:53.380
and fixes its own setup.

218
00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:56.561
Asked, it can usually fetch each vendor's privacy

219
00:09:56.561 --> 00:10:00.543
pages and answer the four questions, dated.

220
00:10:00.543 --> 00:10:03.028
You choose the room, by the output;

221
00:10:03.028 --> 00:10:06.859
local or cloud; the account and its settings;

222
00:10:06.859 --> 00:10:10.226
one dedicated folder, never your home folder.

223
00:10:10.226 --> 00:10:12.083
And the approval level:

224
00:10:12.083 --> 00:10:14.684
current Claude Code starts in Auto,

225
00:10:14.684 --> 00:10:18.016
where a classifier blocks risky actions

226
00:10:18.016 --> 00:10:20.152
instead of asking you.

227
00:10:20.152 --> 00:10:22.044
Ethan Mollick's advice:

228
00:10:22.044 --> 00:10:25.249
until you trust the system, keep it asking.

229
00:10:25.249 --> 00:10:27.838
No agent can tell you whether your employer,

230
00:10:27.838 --> 00:10:31.030
a client contract or a privacy law lets

231
00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:32.969
that data go there.

232
00:10:32.969 --> 00:10:35.187
So before work data goes in,

233
00:10:35.187 --> 00:10:37.729
ask your IT or security admin;

234
00:10:37.729 --> 00:10:40.411
for patient, client or customer data,

235
00:10:40.411 --> 00:10:43.001
whoever owns compliance.

236
00:10:43.001 --> 00:10:44.325
Keep this.

237
00:10:44.325 --> 00:10:46.380
Pick the room by the output:

238
00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:50.199
the model may be shared, but the reach never is.

239
00:10:50.199 --> 00:10:52.835
Ask which computer runs the work:

240
00:10:52.835 --> 00:10:56.561
yours, a box inside it, a machine down the line,

241
00:10:56.561 --> 00:10:59.626
or the vendor's cloud, working on a copy.

242
00:10:59.626 --> 00:11:02.448
Check the account before anything private goes in,

243
00:11:02.448 --> 00:11:05.954
and for work data, ask whoever owns it.

244
00:11:05.954 --> 00:11:09.623
This lesson is built on work by Ethan Mollick,

245
00:11:09.623 --> 00:11:13.779
MIT's Missing Semester, and Anthropic's blog,

246
00:11:13.779 --> 00:11:16.323
help center and terminal guide.
